Store CNG keys created with a name so they survive the process.

Wine's ncrypt keeps a key only for the lifetime of the process that made it:
NCryptCreatePersistedKey ignores the name it is given and NCryptOpenKey is a
stub returning NTE_NOT_SUPPORTED, so a program that asks the key store for the
same key twice gets a different key the second time.

The Epic Games Launcher creates a persistent EC key and signs a DPoP proof with
it (RFC 9449), which is how the account service recognises the machine a token
was issued to.  With the key gone on every start the launcher logs

  LogDPoP: Error: Failed to create persistent DPoP key (Status: 0x80090029)
  LogDPoP: Warning: Cannot build DPoP proof: no public JWK available

and falls back to sending the request unsigned, so the service treats each start
as a new device and issues a new session instead of refreshing the stored one.
After a handful of launches the account hits the issuance limit and every login
fails with

  errors.com.epicgames.account.oauth.too_many_sessions (18048)
  "Sorry too many sessions have been issued for your account."

which no amount of logging out or resetting the password clears, because the
limit counts sessions issued rather than sessions held.

Named keys are now written to %APPDATA%\Microsoft\Crypto\Keys, where Windows
keeps them, once the key is finalized, and read back by NCryptOpenKey; opening a
name that was never stored returns NTE_BAD_KEYSET so callers create one instead
of giving up.  ECDSA P-256 and P-384 are handled alongside RSA, since a key
store that cannot hold the curve DPoP uses would not help here.

A named key that already exists is left alone unless the caller passes
NCRYPT_OVERWRITE_KEY_FLAG, a storage failure is reported by NCryptFinalizeKey
rather than passed off as a finalized key, and the length of a reopened key is
taken from its blob so a 2048 bit RSA key does not come back claiming 1024.

